-- pass/review-reply whole --
**Summary:** The change updates the cache layer so that every key is computed with the sorted
inputs, and the test suite now covers the path. **Details:** The handler (in the server package)
reads all of the inputs, sorts them, and hashes the result: this ensures that one key is produced for
each distinct set of inputs, with the ordering no longer affecting the outcome.
-- hit/review-reply whole --
The change updates the cache layer so that every key is computed from the sorted inputs, and the test
suite now covers the path (both the cold and the warm cache). The handler reads all of the inputs, sorts
them, and hashes the result: this ensures that one key is produced for each distinct set of inputs; the
ordering no longer affects the outcome. The existing behavior is preserved for the other callers (the
CLI, the daemon and the console), and the documentation reflects the new contract: every caller receives
the same result, regardless of the order in which the inputs arrive.

Reviewers should note three points: the hash function is unchanged; the key format is unchanged; and the
migration path (for stored keys) is covered by a new test. Performance is unaffected, and memory use
stays flat across the benchmark suite (small, medium and large graphs). The change also tightens the
error handling: a missing input now fails the build, and the message names the input and the target
that needed it. Finally, the release notes describe the change, and the upgrade guide explains how to
rebuild an older cache (a single command) without losing the stored results; existing caches remain
readable until the next major release, and the deprecation warning names the release that removes them.
